WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.280
Between flubbed lines and technical difficulties,&nbsp;
fans have gotten to see the cast of Breaking Bad's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.134
true colors — and a surprising amount of sexuality&nbsp;
— through the show's official blooper reels.

00:00:10.134 --> 00:00:14.220
Breaking Bad fans will never forget the fallout&nbsp;
from Hank's fateful trip to the bathroom, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:19.020
he finally realizes Heisbenberg's true identity.&nbsp;
When the second part of Season 5 debuted, Hank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:22.732
played by Dean Norris, wastes no time&nbsp;
confronting Walt about his double life.

00:00:22.732 --> 00:00:25.912
While the tense standoff between&nbsp;
Hank and Walt is unforgettable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.912 --> 00:00:28.500
you might not know that to get&nbsp;
this über-serious moment right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.980
the Breaking Bad crew had to overcome&nbsp;
some pretty hilarious technical issues.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.713
In the bloopers for the second half of Season&nbsp;
5, we see the attempts at this scene gone wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.713 --> 00:00:40.860
At the moment when Hank pushes the button to&nbsp;
threateningly close the garage door, it repeatedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.284
gets stuck. Norris keeps jamming the button to&nbsp;
try and get the door to shut but to no avail.

00:00:45.284 --> 00:00:48.780
At one point, Norris gives up and drops&nbsp;
the garage door remote on the table to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:53.160
end a take before dramatically falling over.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, by that point, neither actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.580
seemed to be able to keep it together to&nbsp;
get through a take without cracking up.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:01.020
This blooper is a Bryan Cranston classic and&nbsp;
one of the rare hilarious moments that came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.860
from the actor staying in character rather&nbsp;
than breaking it. In this Season 4 outtake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.980
the filmmakers were trying to capture a&nbsp;
low-angle shot of Walt sticking a bill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.360
into a vending machine. It's a small shot you&nbsp;
probably don't remember, but when Cranston&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.510
can't quite seem to get the machine to take&nbsp;
the dollar, an unforgettable moment erupts.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.440
In the take, Cranston feeds the bill&nbsp;
into the machine multiple times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.820
only for it to be spat back out at the actor.&nbsp;
After the machine repeatedly rejects him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:27.060
Cranston, still in character, mutters&nbsp;
a certain name under his breath.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.348
"Bogdan."

00:01:28.348 --> 00:01:30.960
The man he's referring to is the&nbsp;
original owner of the carwash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.500
Since Walt and Bogdan have never had&nbsp;
a particularly friendly relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.640
it's no surprise that Walt would blame Bogdan for&nbsp;
not keeping his vending machines in working order.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.260
In a characteristically manipulative&nbsp;
moment from Season 2, Walt hypes Jesse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.200
up by likening him to a blowfish. In his defense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:51.120
it's an apt metaphor. As Walter says, both&nbsp;
Jesse and the blowfish start out unassuming.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:57.300
"Small in stature, not swift, not&nbsp;
cunning. Easy prey for predators."

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.140
The point of this comparison was to highlight&nbsp;
Jesse's ability to intimidate others when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.560
needs to, even if he appears weak&nbsp;
on the outside. The speech works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.820
and Jesse is noticeably more energized for the&nbsp;
rest of the scene. However, in the Breaking Bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:13.080
Season 2 bloopers, we see what happens when&nbsp;
Bryan Cranston can't quite nail the line.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.220
The final line of the speech is the rhetorical&nbsp;
question, "Who messes with the blowfish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.952
Jesse?" In the outtakes, Cranston slips up&nbsp;
by repeatedly calling Jesse the wrong name.

00:02:21.952 --> 00:02:24.112
"Who messes with the blowfish, Jeff?"

00:02:24.112 --> 00:02:29.460
If you're wondering who Jeff could possibly&nbsp;
be, even Cranston himself doesn't know. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.000
the blooper continues, we see him rack his&nbsp;
brain to try and figure out just where that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.260
came from. We may never know the answer, but we&nbsp;
are thankful for this famous flub regardless.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.540
This behind-the-scenes moment shows us the light&nbsp;
side of Breaking Bad star Anna Gunn. While Skyler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:46.020
White was pretty much all business all the&nbsp;
time during Gunn's scenes on the drama, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.920
clear from this Season 2 blooper that the actress&nbsp;
who played her has a much better sense of humor.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.760
In this series of clips, we see Gunn&nbsp;
on set holding a prosthetic hand. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:57.420
she feels herself up with the hand, the&nbsp;
actress jokingly moans Tuco's name before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.720
throwing her head back and cackling&nbsp;
at the absurdity of what she is doing.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.260
Bryan Cranston and Aaron Paul&nbsp;
got very close throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.240
shooting of Breaking Bad. So close, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.020
that their friendship has extended well into&nbsp;
the decade since the series's last season aired.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:14.280
Paul and Cranston's chemistry both on and off&nbsp;
screen was so natural that Jesse's original fate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.480
was even written out of the show to keep him on&nbsp;
as a main character. However, if you haven't seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.920
this particular outtake, you might not realize&nbsp;
just how close the two were. For as close as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.700
the two were emotionally, some viewers might be&nbsp;
surprised how close they've gotten physically.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.800
In this Breaking Bad Season 2 blooper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.880
the actors who play Walt and Jesse have an&nbsp;
intimate moment of male bonding. In this clip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.140
the two are embracing when Paul reaches for&nbsp;
Cranston's butt and starts to rub his co-star.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.200
The actors start to verbally flirt&nbsp;
with one another while they strip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.440
off their hazard suits. This sensual&nbsp;
moment might change the way you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:46.740
at Walt and Jesse's relationship the&nbsp;
next time you rewatch Breaking Bad.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.960
When most viewers think of Breaking Bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.980
they think of one iconically menacing&nbsp;
line spoken by Walter White in Season 4.

00:03:53.169 --> 00:03:56.949
"I am not in danger, Skyler, I am the danger."

00:03:56.949 --> 00:04:01.200
As legendary as this scene is, Cranston and&nbsp;
Gunn didn't get it on every take. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.100
the showrunners included some of the mishaps&nbsp;
from this scene in the Season 4 blooper reel.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:09.780
In this clip, Gunn tees Cranston up perfectly,&nbsp;
delivering her line about Walt being "in danger"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:14.340
with absolute sincerity. The response starts&nbsp;
as a smile, but before Cranston can break into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.060
the main part of his speech, he starts&nbsp;
laughing uncontrollably. Something about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:22.020
the moment was just too much for Cranston.&nbsp;
We all laugh in uncomfortable situations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:26.520
but there's something particularly bizarre&nbsp;
about seeing Walter White break in that moment.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:30.960
In one of the most outlandish moments of his&nbsp;
entire career, Bryan Cranston let it all loose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:35.520
while filming a shower scene for Breaking Bad&nbsp;
Season 4. In addition to being completely naked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.540
and in the middle of shaving his head, this&nbsp;
blooper depicts the disrobed Cranston singing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:43.878
a SpongeBob SquarePants theme song parody. While&nbsp;
waiting for the showrunners to get the right shot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.878 --> 00:04:47.435
Cranston chooses to adopt a pirate&nbsp;
accent before singing to himself.

00:04:47.624 --> 00:04:51.173
"Who works naked with the light meter?"

00:04:51.173 --> 00:04:54.780
The answer is not entirely clear, although&nbsp;
you can definitely make out that whoever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:58.980
this shanty is about is known for their,&nbsp;
quote, "sweaty balls." As he continues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.880
he backs up into the frame, where, if not&nbsp;
for the mercy of the editors, viewers would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.668
have become even more intimately familiar with&nbsp;
Cranston's body than the Pilot episode allows.

00:05:07.668 --> 00:05:12.180
We've already seen how, in the face of technical&nbsp;
difficulties, Brian Cranston can improvise silly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:16.020
contributions to scenes that still make some&nbsp;
sense in the context of the show. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:20.280
his interaction with Gus Fring actor&nbsp;
Giancarlo Esposito is anything but sensible.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:25.500
In this outtake from Breaking Bad Season 4, Bryan&nbsp;
Cranston improvises between takes by ordering a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.940
meal at the register while Esposito takes his&nbsp;
order. While a tense conversation should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:34.200
transpiring in these moments, Cranston just&nbsp;
keeps telling Esposito his order. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.220
Cranston tells him, in a top-tier&nbsp;
deadpan, about his dietary restrictions.

00:05:38.518 --> 00:05:40.656
"No, no. I'm allergic to tacos."

00:05:40.656 --> 00:05:45.060
In the lab where they cook meth, Walt and Jesse&nbsp;
are often seen transporting large plastic boxes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:48.960
and barrels filled with various chemicals.&nbsp;
Occasionally, these barrels have been filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.940
with bodies dissolving in acid. Having disposed of&nbsp;
a body by doing the same thing early in Season 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:57.420
it seems the pair have just gotten used&nbsp;
to this morbid occupational hazard.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:01.080
This aspect of their job adds a stark&nbsp;
contrast to this Season 4 blooper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:04.140
where Cranston and Paul are seen playing&nbsp;
with an empty one of these barrels.

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:08.760
The clip begins with Cranston holding a large&nbsp;
plastic barrel by the lid and slamming it on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.660
the floor in an attempt to open the top.&nbsp;
Paul comes in to start pounding on it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:16.140
then Cranston gives it a large kick.&nbsp;
It's not the most outstanding of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:20.040
Season 4 bloopers but it does provide a&nbsp;
unique look behind the show's curtain.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:24.540
This blooper comes off like Bryan Cranston&nbsp;
doing a comedy sketch. In the same vein as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:27.360
his shower song, we weren't quite&nbsp;
sure what the actor was on that day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:30.480
but we appreciate his improvisational&nbsp;
antics even more this time around.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:34.260
In this clip from the third Season&nbsp;
4 blooper reel, Cranston delivers a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:39.720
satire of a PSA directly to the camera. What&nbsp;
is it for? Anal bleaching, as it turns out.

00:06:40.185 --> 00:06:43.545
"From the cast to the crew: an anal bleaching kit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:47.880
Because no crew should walk&nbsp;
around with a dark anus."

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:51.780
Walt and Skyler's marital life has always&nbsp;
been complicated, to say the least. Between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:55.680
the constant power struggle, the lies, and&nbsp;
the cheating, it's far from a typical married&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.000
relationship. Even so, some elements of their&nbsp;
romantic life remained a mystery to the audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.840
throughout the run of the series — at least&nbsp;
until the Season 4 blooper reels came out.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.460
In this outtake from Season 4, Bryan Cranston&nbsp;
walks up to Anna Gunn while clothed in a baby&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:12.420
costume, diaper and all, before taking&nbsp;
a pacifier out of his mouth to speak.

00:07:12.534 --> 00:07:16.134
"I know it was my fantasy, but&nbsp;
I hope you got some enjoyment."

00:07:16.134 --> 00:07:21.000
Gunn laughs and cheekily calls Cranston a "good&nbsp;
boy" before giving her co-star a playful spank.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:26.040
In the final season of Breaking Bad, audiences saw&nbsp;
the long-awaited return of some characters that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:29.460
had been out of focus for several seasons.&nbsp;
In one scene, we see Jesse's old friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:33.840
Badger and Skinny Pete, up to their old nonsense&nbsp;
while running an errand for Jesse and Walt.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:37.740
This time, we see them messing around with&nbsp;
the instruments in a music store. As Badger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:41.580
played by Matt Jones, hammers away on&nbsp;
the guitar to no avail, we see Pete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:45.120
played by Charles Baker, turn out a&nbsp;
nice classical melody on the piano.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.480
While these events might already sound&nbsp;
familiar to those who have seen the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:53.400
this Season 5 blooper shows a longer clip,&nbsp;
featuring an extended piano solo by Skinny Pete.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:57.540
Baker continues his classical demonstration&nbsp;
long after Jones stops strumming and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:01.920
take ends. As he plays, Jones looks on in&nbsp;
frustration until Baker finishes his moment.

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:06.300
"Yeah, we get it, you're better."

